{"id":"lobbied-measures-by-subject","title":"Enacted ordinances matched to lobbying by subject (an inference)","question":"Which enacted ordinances were lobbied on where the lobbyist gave no file number, judging by the words of the subject?","finding":"Matching the subject of each contact to the titles of 953 ordinances enacted from 2018 to 2026 ties a further 250 contacts to 36 ordinances. This is an inference from wording, kept apart from the file-number matches. For 6 of them the lobbying circle gave to a sponsor’s committees; the clients paid the firms involved $3.1 million in the years of contact. Most contacts: Planning Code - Jobs Housing Linkage Fee and Inclusionary Housing (33 contacts).","how":"For contacts with no usable file number, the words of the “issue” are compared with each ordinance’s short title and title digest. A match needs: at least two words shared with the short title; shared words carrying at least three-quarters of the subject’s weight (rare words weigh more); no distinctive word of the subject missing from the ordinance; a street number in common or the shared words covering at least 35% of the short title; the contact dated between one year before introduction and thirty days after passage; and one ordinance clearly ahead of any other. The last column shows the words that matched. Everything else is computed as in the file-number screen.","limits":"A subject match is a guess, not a statement by the lobbyist: two measures can share a street address or a phrase, and a contact about a project can be matched to a later ordinance about the same site that the client did not seek. Only enacted ordinances are in the list, so measures that failed cannot appear and the pass rate means nothing here. Lobbying is registered, and so is giving by a lobbyist’s clients and colleagues. An official can sponsor or vote for something a donor wants because the official already favoured it; a donor can give to an official because of what the official already stands for. Amounts, parties and dates taken from Board file titles are read by machine: in a hand check of 50 titles, 11 had at least one field read wrongly (4 after later fixes).","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"file","t":"Board file","type":"link"},{"k":"title","t":"Short title","type":"text"},{"k":"type","t":"Type","type":"text"},{"k":"outcome","t":"Outcome on record","type":"text"},{"k":"amount","t":"Amount in the file","type":"usd"},{"k":"clients","t":"Clients","type":"text"},{"k":"firms","t":"Firms","type":"text"},{"k":"asked","t":"Contacts by position sought","type":"text"},{"k":"contacts","t":"Contacts","type":"int"},{"k":"electedContacted","t":"Elected officials contacted","type":"text"},{"k":"sponsors","t":"Sponsors","type":"text"},{"k":"sponsorContacted","t":"A sponsor was contacted","type":"text"},{"k":"vote","t":"Final Board vote, ayes to noes","type":"text"},{"k":"noes","t":"Members voting no","type":"text"},{"k":"fees","t":"Clients paid these firms in the years of contact","type":"usd"},{"k":"giveSponsors","t":"Circle gave the sponsors (two years before introduction to passage)","type":"usd"},{"k":"giveYes","t":"Circle gave members who voted yes (two years before)","type":"usd"},{"k":"words","t":"Matched on the words","type":"text"}],"rows":[{"file":{"t":"190548","u":"https://sfgov.legistar.com/LegislationDetail.aspx?ID=3950583&GUID=E416740A-B76F-49B6-BD44-EAB578E96A40"},"title":"Planning Code - Jobs Housing Linkage Fee and Inclusionary Housing","type":"Ordinance","outcome":"Passed 2019-11-15; version 4 at the end","amount":null,"clients":"San Francisco Chamber of Commerce; Boston Properties","firms":"San Francisco Association of Realtors; San Francisco Chamber of Commerce and 1 more","asked":"21 monitor or unclear","contacts":21,"electedContacted":"Matt Haney; Aaron Peskin; Rafael Mandelman and 2 more","sponsors":"Matt Haney; Sandra Lee Fewer; Hillary Ronen and 7 more","sponsorContacted":"yes","vote":"10 to 0 (Finally Passed, 2019-11-05)","noes":"